INTRODUCTORY NOTE ON HEIGHTENED PLEADING STANDARDS
Rules and statutes occasionally go beyond the Rule 8(a) pleading standard and hold certain types of allegations to a heightened standard of pleading. Rule 9(b), for example, places additional requirements on pleading in certain cases. Under Rule 9(b), when a plaintiff alleges fraud or mistake as part of a claim, she “must state with particularity the circumstances constituting fraud or mistake.” However, “[m]alice, intent, knowledge, and other conditions of a person’s mind may be alleged generally.”

Some statutes also set heightened standards. For example, the Private Securities Litigation Reform Act of 1995 (PSLRA) was enacted to guard against strike suits. Strike suit is a nickname for a lawsuit alleging violations of the security laws (for example, laws prohibiting corporations from misrepresenting their profits to shareholders) filed not based on evidence of corporate misconduct, but merely to extract a nuisance settlement—in other words, a settlement that a defendant pays because it is cheaper to settle (even for millions of dollars) than to defend against the (allegedly frivolous) claim. The PSLRA requires plaintiffs alleging violations of securities law “to state with particularity both the facts constituting the alleged violation, and the facts evidencing scienter, i.e., the defendant’s intention ‘to deceive, manipulate, or defraud.’ ” Tellabs Inc., v.
